The cards are posted without another human review. Do not add new facts; fix only what is wrong, and leave correct parts as they are.

## Checklist

1. Numbers: every number must be a value from the market data, sector or stock tables (or that value rounded to a whole number). Remove numbers that are not in the tables (article figures, computed figures) and write the sentence without them. Checklist levels (e.g. "S&P 500 at 7,000") are fine. Digits inside names (Nikkei 225, S&P 500) are not numbers.
2. Level vs change: never let a level read like a change. "USD/KRW up 1,356" reads as a 1,356 move; write "USD/KRW rose to 1,356" or "USD/KRW up 1.21%".
3. Name the market (the KOSPI, the Nasdaq), never a city. Every sentence says what moved, by how much, which way.
4. Vague lines: rewrite or remove metaphors, cryptic lines and anything a reader cannot act on.
5. Events: keep only events supported by the articles or the blog summary whose detail clearly says what to check. Drop weak or vague ones.
6. Reasons: if an article does not support why something moved, keep the fact and drop the reason.
7. Fix grammar, spelling and awkward phrasing.
8. Length: headline lines about 20 characters, paragraphs 25-35 words, "why it matters" about 20 words.
9. Remove calls to action (save, follow, link in bio) and any investment advice.
